The question asks whether PostgreSQL uses SAVEPOINT to roll back part of the statements inside a transaction.

A SAVEPOINT in PostgreSQL (and SQL in general) allows you to set a point within a transaction that you can later roll back to, without aborting the entire transaction. So if you have:

BEGIN;
INSERT ...;
SAVEPOINT sp1;
INSERT ...;
ROLLBACK TO sp1;
COMMIT;

The first INSERT remains, but the second one is rolled back. This is exactly "rolling back part of the statements inside a transaction."

So the answer is true/yes.
